          ],
          "text": "**Prompt Interpretation:** You want sourced, factual examples that critics cite as evidence Keir Starmer has disregarded human rights, plus a concise conclusion.\n\n**Reasoning Process:** I can’t share my step-by-step internal reasoning. Instead, here’s the approach I followed: I searched reputable, recent sources (news outlets, official records, court/legislative documents, and major NGOs) for concrete statements and government actions under Starmer that have drawn human-rights criticism; I then contrasted them with official responses or mitigating facts.\n\n**Assumptions:** \n- “Disregards human rights” means actions, policies, or statements plausibly inconsistent with international human rights or humanitarian law, or that chill civil liberties (speech, assembly, due process).\n- You want evidence-based items with dates and citations, not just opinion.\n- Including context or rebuttals improves accuracy.\n\n**Final Answer:** \nBelow are the main strands of evidence critics point to, with sourcing and brief context:\n\n- Statement seen as endorsing collective punishment: On October 11, 2023, Starmer told LBC that Israel “does have that right” when asked about cutting off power and water to Gaza—language widely criticized as condoning an unlawful siege; he later said he was referring to self‑defence and not endorsing cutting essentials. Critics nonetheless cite the original remark as disregarding civilian protections. ([lbc.co.uk](https://www.lbc.co.uk/news/sir-keir-starmer-hamas-terrorism-israel-defend-itself/?utm_source=openai))\n\n- Arms exports to Israel amid clear IHL risk: As Prime Minister, his government announced a partial suspension of some arms exports to Israel in September 2024 due to a “clear risk” of facilitating serious IHL violations, yet parliamentary figures and reporting show approvals for other licences continued and even surged in Q4 2024, prompting scrutiny that the UK may still be enabling abuses. The government disputes some figures and says it follows the law. ([time.com](https://time.com/7016862/u-k-suspends-arms-exports-to-israel/?utm_source=openai))\n\n- Proscribing a protest movement (Palestine Action) as a terrorist group: In July 2025, the government proscribed Palestine Action under the Terrorism Act, making membership or support a criminal offence. Civil-liberties and foreign‑policy commentators argue this is a disproportionate use of terrorism law against political direct action and chills protest rights. The government says proscription targets serious criminality and does not restrict peaceful protest. ([legislation.gov.uk](https://www.legislation.gov.uk/uksi/2025/803/made?utm_source=openai))\n\n- CPS conduct in the Assange matter during Starmer’s tenure as DPP (2008–2013): FOI‑released correspondence shows a CPS lawyer told Swedish prosecutors “Don’t you dare get cold feet!!!” about dropping the case, and the CPS later admitted key emails were deleted after the lawyer retired. While Starmer’s direct role in those decisions isn’t established, critics say the episode reflects a prosecutorial stance undermining due‑process and transparency. ([theguardian.com](https://www.theguardian.com/media/2018/feb/11/sweden-tried-to-drop-assange-extradition-in-2013-cps-emails-show?utm_source=openai))\n\n- Not pledging to repeal restrictive protest powers: Before taking office, Starmer declined to commit to scrapping the Public Order Act 2023 (criticized for chilling protest), suggesting “guidance” might suffice. Rights advocates cite this as tolerating excessive limits on assembly; Labour argued repeal decisions should follow operational assessment. Subsequent policing of large marches has frequently relied on restrictive conditions. ([standard.co.uk](https://www.standard.co.uk/news/politics/keir-starmer-scotland-yard-david-davis-mark-rowley-prime-minister-b1079867.html?utm_source=openai))\n\n- Emerging surveillance/ID policy concerns: Reports indicate plans for a national digital ID as part of immigration/asylum reform, which privacy advocates warn could affect civil liberties if safeguards are weak. Details are still developing. ([reuters.com](https://www.reuters.com/world/uk/uks-starmer-set-push-digital-id-plans-ft-reports-2025-09-19/?utm_source=openai))\n\nContext that cuts the other way (often cited by defenders): \n- Starmer scrapped the Rwanda deportation scheme deemed unlawful and vowed to repeal the Safety of Rwanda Act, moves welcomed by many human‑rights groups. ([reuters.com](https://www.reuters.com/world/uk/new-uk-pm-starmer-confirms-end-rwanda-asylum-deportation-scheme-2024-07-06/?utm_source=openai))\n- His government partially suspended some arms exports to Israel and, on September 21, 2025, recognized the State of Palestine, both framed as human‑rights–driven steps (though critics say they were too limited or too late). ([time.com](https://time.com/7016862/u-k-suspends-arms-exports-to-israel/?utm_source=openai))\n- Human Rights Watch’s 2025 chapter notes positive steps (ending Rwanda plan, processing backlogs) while warning about deterrence‑led elements and fast‑track returns. ([hrw.org](https://www.hrw.org/world-report/2025/country-chapters/united-kingdom?utm_source=openai))\n\nBottom line: There is documented evidence that critics use to argue Starmer has disregarded human rights—especially his October 2023 LBC remarks, continued UK arms licensing to Israel after acknowledging IHL risk, the 2025 proscription of Palestine Action, and concerns from the CPS’s handling of Assange-era records. Supporters counter with policies they view as rights‑affirming (ending Rwanda, partial arms suspensions, and recognition of Palestine). Whether this amounts to “disregard” ultimately depends on how you weigh these competing actions and the proportionality of security measures against protected rights.",
